# Deploying {{ project_name }}

`.github/workflows/deploy.yml` is the only thing that deploys this project. It
sequences stages and holds no logic of its own; every stage is a composite
action under `.github/actions/`, and each one is runnable and testable on its
own. Their tests are the `pipeline-stages` CI job.

Nothing here has AWS keys. The workflow assumes a role over OIDC, per
environment, and the roles themselves are created outside this repository.

## Triggering it

`workflow_dispatch` only — never `on: push`. A staging deploy is chained by
the orchestrator after a green build; a production deploy is a human pressing
**Ship It** in Jira. Neither of those is "someone merged something".

| Input | Required | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `environment` | yes | `staging` or `production`. Mirrors `terraform/variables.tf`'s validation. |
| `ref` | no | Git ref to deploy. **Staging only** — see below. Defaults to the ref the run was dispatched against. |

```bash
gh workflow run deploy.yml --field environment=staging
gh workflow run deploy.yml --ref main --field environment=production
```

### Why `ref` is refused for production

"Only `main` deploys to production" is enforced by AWS rather than by this
workflow: the production role's OIDC trust policy pins
`repo:<org>/<repo>:ref:refs/heads/main`, so a run dispatched against any other
ref cannot assume it at all.

That pin is built from `github.ref` — the ref the run was *dispatched*
against. The `ref` **input** is a different value, read only by
`actions/checkout`. Supplying both would mean dispatching against `main` to
satisfy the pin, then building something else entirely, and AWS could not see
the difference. It is the one part of the guarantee AWS structurally cannot
enforce, so the workflow refuses it before assuming any role.

Rolling back by redeploying an older commit is out of scope for now
(`docs/cd-pipeline.md` says fix forward), which is why the refusal is flat
rather than "must be an ancestor of `main`".

Two repository **variables** (not secrets — a role ARN is not one) select the
role: `AWS_ROLE_STAGING` and `AWS_ROLE_PROD`. A missing one fails the run with
a message rather than falling back to the other environment's role.

Deploys are serialised per environment by a `concurrency` group. A second run
queues rather than racing the Terraform state lock, and a queued run costs no
Actions minutes.

## The stages

| Stage | Capability | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `capacity-gate` | HOSTED | Sums provisioned DynamoDB capacity across every table in the account and region, and fails above a threshold (default 20 of the 25-unit always-free allowance). |
| `ensure-secrets` | HOSTED | Creates `/{{ project_slug }}/<environment>/session_secret` as a SecureString **if it is absent**. Never rotates. |
| `terraform-apply` | HOSTED | `init` against this environment's state key, `plan` to a file, `apply` that file. |
{% if needs_datastore %}
| `seed` | DATABASE | Reseeds the staging table from a sanitized export of the production table. Staging only. |
{% endif %}
| `smoke-test` | HOSTED | Polls the deployed health path with bounded backoff and fails the job if it never answers. |

### Why `capacity-gate` runs even without a datastore

DynamoDB's always-free allowance is 25 read and 25 write units shared by every
table in the whole account. `terraform-modules`' `cost_acknowledged`
precondition sees one table's own numbers, so five projects can each pass at 6
units and collectively sit at 30. This stage is the only place that sees the
sum, so it runs on every hosted deploy — including a deploy of a project that
owns no tables, because the account it is deploying into is the same account.

### Why `ensure-secrets` never rotates

Regenerating the session secret on every deploy invalidates every active
session, so everyone is logged out each time the project ships. It is created
once per environment and left alone after that; rotation is a deliberate act —
delete the parameter and redeploy.
{% if needs_datastore %}

### `seed` refuses until this project supplies a sanitizer

Production data may only reach staging through `scripts/sanitize-seed.js`, a
file this project writes. The template cannot know which attributes are
personal data, and a seed stage that silently copies real data is worse than
no seed stage.

Until that file exists the stage copies **nothing**, says so as a warning and
in the job summary, and lets the deploy continue — staging runs against an
empty table. It does not fail the deploy, because staging with no data is a
working staging. Start from `scripts/sanitize-seed.example.js`.

The sanitizer takes one item in DynamoDB's `AttributeValue` form and returns a
new item to keep, or `null` to drop it. Returning the object it was handed is
refused: `item => item` copies production verbatim and nothing else could tell
it apart from a real sanitizer.

Export is a `Scan`, not a point-in-time export — PITR bills per GB-month and
scanning a small table costs a handful of read units. Items are sanitized page
by page, so raw production data is never accumulated, never written to disk and
never uploaded as an artifact.
{% endif %}

## `deploy-result.json` — the result contract

Every run uploads an artifact named **`deploy-result`** containing a single
file, `deploy-result.json`. It is written on the failure path as well as the
success path, because a failed deploy still has to report something.

```json
{
  "environment": "staging",
  "url": "https://abc123.lambda-url.{{ aws_region }}.on.aws/",
  "sha": "0f4c1d2e...",
  "status": "success"
}
```

| Field | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `environment` | string | Exactly the `environment` input. |
| `url` | string | The deployed Function URL. **Empty** when the run failed before `terraform-apply` produced one. |
| `sha` | string | The commit that was actually deployed — `git rev-parse HEAD` after checkout, not the dispatching sha, which differ whenever `ref` is supplied. |
| `status` | string | `success`, `failure` or `cancelled`. |

**This is a consumed contract.** The orchestrator polls the run and reads this
artifact to comment on the Jira ticket, so the four key names are an interface
with something outside this repository. Add fields freely; do not rename or
remove one without changing its consumer.

A named artifact rather than job outputs, because reading a job's outputs back
across the REST API is awkward while an artifact is one download with a stable
name.

## What the deploy role needs

Beyond the permissions Terraform needs to manage this project's own resources:

| Action | Scope | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| `dynamodb:ListTables` | `*` — the point is that it is account-wide | `capacity-gate` |
| `dynamodb:DescribeTable` | `*` | `capacity-gate` |
| `ssm:GetParameter`, `ssm:PutParameter` | `/{{ project_slug }}/<environment>/*` | `ensure-secrets` |
| `s3:GetObject`, `s3:PutObject`, `s3:DeleteObject` | the shared state bucket, `{{ project_slug }}/*` prefix only | `terraform-apply` |
| `s3:ListBucket` | the shared state bucket | `terraform-apply` |
{% if needs_datastore %}
| `dynamodb:Scan` | `{{ project_slug }}-production-items` | `seed`, from the **staging** role |
| `dynamodb:Scan`, `dynamodb:BatchWriteItem`, `dynamodb:DescribeTable` | `{{ project_slug }}-staging-items` | `seed` |
{% endif %}

`ensure-secrets` deliberately does not decrypt the parameter it checks for —
it only asks whether it exists — so the deploy role needs no `kms:Decrypt`.
{% if needs_datastore %}

Note the one asymmetry: seeding means the **staging** role can read the
production table. That is inherent to seeding staging from production, and it
is why the permission is scoped to `Scan` on that one table rather than to the
production prefix.
{% endif %}

## Running one stage by itself

Each stage is a directory under `.github/actions/` with its own `action.yml`,
so a throwaway workflow can call just one:

```yaml
- uses: ./.github/actions/capacity-gate
  with:
    threshold: '20'
```

The stages that are more than a few lines of shell are Node scripts with no
dependencies, so they also run directly:

```bash
node .github/actions/capacity-gate/capacity.js --threshold 20
```

Their tests live beside them and run as CI's `pipeline-stages` job:

```bash
npm test --prefix .github/actions
```
